      <h2>The Thai Keto Pantry: What Stays, What Goes</h2>
      <p>Thai cooking relies on a specific set of ingredients that create its signature flavor profile — hot, sour, salty, and sweet. The good news for keto cooks is that three of those four elements (hot, sour, salty) are naturally low-carb. It's only the "sweet" that needs adaptation. This guide covers every major Thai ingredient category and how to navigate it on keto.</p>

      <h2>Sauces: The Flavor Foundation</h2>
      <h3>Keto-Friendly (Use Freely)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fish sauce (nam pla):</strong> 0g carbs. The single most important Thai seasoning. Made from fermented anchovies and salt. Red Boat, Three Crabs, and Squid are excellent brands.</li>
        <li><strong>Coconut aminos:</strong> 1g net carb per tsp. The best soy sauce alternative for keto. Slightly sweeter than soy sauce, which actually works well in Thai dishes.</li>
        <li><strong>Shrimp paste (kapi):</strong> 0g carbs. Fermented shrimp paste used in curry pastes and nam prik (chili dips). A little goes a long way.</li>
        <li><strong>Rice vinegar:</strong> 0g carbs. Unseasoned rice vinegar is carb-free. Avoid "seasoned" rice vinegar which contains sugar.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Use Sparingly (Moderate Carbs)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Tamarind paste:</strong> ~3g net carbs per tbsp. Essential for Pad Thai and some soups. The carbs are worth it for authenticity — just measure carefully.</li>
        <li><strong>Oyster sauce:</strong> ~3g net carbs per tbsp. Adds deep umami to stir-fries. Look for brands without added sugar, or use half the amount.</li>
        <li><strong>Nam prik pao (Thai chili paste):</strong> ~2g net carbs per tsp. Roasted chili paste with shrimp. Adds smoky depth to tom yum. Use sparingly.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Avoid or Substitute</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Sweet soy sauce (kecap manis):</strong> Almost entirely sugar. Substitute with coconut aminos + a few drops of liquid stevia.</li>
        <li><strong>Hoisin sauce:</strong> Very high in sugar. Omit or make a keto version with peanut butter, coconut aminos, and erythritol.</li>
        <li><strong>Sweet chili sauce:</strong> Pure sugar. Make your own with erythritol, rice vinegar, and chili flakes.</li>
        <li><strong>Plum sauce:</strong> Sugar-based. Skip entirely.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Herbs & Aromatics: The Soul of Thai Cooking</h2>
      <p>Almost all Thai herbs and aromatics are keto-friendly with negligible carbs. This is where Thai cuisine shines for keto — you can use these freely to build incredible flavor.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Lemongrass:</strong> 0g net carbs. Citrusy, floral. Bruise and add whole to soups and curries.</li>
        <li><strong>Galangal:</strong> 0g net carbs. Piney, citrusy ginger relative. Essential for tom yum and tom kha.</li>
        <li><strong>Kaffir lime leaves:</strong> 0g net carbs. The most important Thai aromatic. Freezes beautifully.</li>
        <li><strong>Thai basil (horapha):</strong> 0g net carbs. Anise-like. Essential for green curry and pad kra pao.</li>
        <li><strong>Cilantro (pak chee):</strong> 0g net carbs. Use leaves, stems, and roots.</li>
        <li><strong>Mint (saranae):</strong> 0g net carbs. For larb and Thai salads.</li>
        <li><strong>Garlic:</strong> ~1g net carb per clove. Use freely — the flavor payoff is worth the minimal carbs.</li>
        <li><strong>Thai chilies:</strong> 0g net carbs. Bird chilies, prik chee fa (spur chilies), and dried chili flakes.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Sweeteners: Replacing Palm Sugar</h2>
      <p>Palm sugar is the traditional Thai sweetener, but it's pure sugar. Here are the best keto substitutes ranked by how well they work in Thai cooking:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Erythritol (granulated):</strong> Best all-around. Dissolves well, no aftertaste, and the slight cooling effect is barely noticeable in spicy dishes.</li>
        <li><strong>Monk fruit sweetener (granulated):</strong> Excellent. No aftertaste, no cooling effect. More expensive but worth it.</li>
        <li><strong>Allulose:</strong> Closest to real sugar in behavior. Caramelizes and dissolves like sugar. Slightly less sweet — use 1.3x the amount.</li>
        <li><strong>Liquid stevia:</strong> Good for sauces and dressings. A few drops go a long way. Can have a bitter aftertaste if overused.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Where to Buy Thai Keto Ingredients</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Asian grocery stores:</strong> Best for fresh herbs (lemongrass, galangal, kaffir lime, Thai basil), curry pastes, and fish sauce. Prices are usually much better than online.</li>
        <li><strong>Amazon:</strong> Great for coconut aminos, konjac noodles, erythritol, and hard-to-find curry pastes. Check out our <a href="/roundups">product roundups</a> for specific recommendations.</li>
        <li><strong>Regular supermarkets:</strong> Many now carry coconut milk, fish sauce, and even Thai curry pastes in the international aisle.</li>
        <li><strong>Freezer section:</strong> Frozen lemongrass, galangal, and kaffir lime leaves are excellent alternatives to fresh and keep for months.</li>
      </ul>
